WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.820
Hello, everyone.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.880
It's Wednesday night,
and I just sat down

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.320
at my mom's bar, Sneakers.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.720
I can hear the band
doing their soundcheck

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.360
and I take a bite of
my Buffalo chicken

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.060
and I look across the
bar and see my mom sit

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.310
down next to a regular.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:26.710
She strikes up a
conversation with them

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:29.710
and introduces them to my
neighbors sitting a few seats

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.450
down.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.490
I've witnessed
this so many times

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:39.180
at Sneakers that it's
become commonplace to me.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.660
But lately, probably
the last year or so,

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.060
I've thought a lot about
the community building

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.440
that she's done

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.920
so naturally the past 38 years.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:56.740
And when I say growing up in a
restaurant, I really mean that.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:01.000
This is my baby picture on
the bar there at Sneakers.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:07.210
But jumping ahead, I'd say
just about 30 years from there,

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:10.810
I am now our manager of
our professional community

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:13.340
at RStudio.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.820
So in my role, I get to
work with a lot of you all,

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:20.160
either in the room or
watching virtually.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.890
And help connect customers
to be able to share

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:28.170
use cases and best
practices and teach lessons

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.500
learned with one another.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.100
In doing so, I've learned
that a lot of what

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.820
makes us really good
at our job is not

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.840
what's apparent in our resume
or through our prior job

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:48.540
experience or through
the degree that we have.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.650
So when I think about my role
in organizing and building

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.860
community at
RStudio, I realize

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.185
I've been running my own bar.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:01.610
There are four,

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:03.520
well, there's so
many things that you

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.320
can learn from restaurant
experience itself.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.910
But there are four key takeaways
that I want to share with you

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:13.690
all today that you
can take in building

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.600
communities of
your own or growing

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.060
communities of your own.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:28.150
So the first one, find your
Taco Tuesday or be consistent.

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:31.640
At Sneakers and at a
lot of restaurants.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:35.500
I'm sure we know there are
certain days that are often

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:41.080
associated with certain
foods or certain days that

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.200
are associated with
certain events at Sneakers.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.910
this could be having
tacos on Tuesday

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:52.770
or live music on
Wednesday and Saturday.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.750
And people don't come
to every single event,

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:01.810
but when they have
a free night or when

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:04.210
they're looking for
something to do with friends,

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:07.870
they know exactly
where they can go.

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:13.770
And so I try to do this with
our events at RStudio as well.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:17.250
So every Tuesday
at 12 Eastern time,

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:22.050
I host the RStudio
Enterprise Community meetup.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:24.720
And this is a meetup
event for people

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.910
to just share what they're doing
so they may share a new use

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:35.490
case or a best practice learned
and present for 30 minutes

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:39.060
and take questions
from the community.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.050
And so these happen every
Tuesday at 12 Eastern time.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:46.740
And it may be people gathering
around an industry group

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:51.090
like using R in health
care or more broadly using

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:54.880
Shiny and production.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:58.840
Also every Thursday
at 12 Eastern time,

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:03.610
I host our data science hangout,
which I see a few people here

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.860
who have been to those as well.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:08.900
This is a little
bit less formal.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.720
Not that the other
is formal either,

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.890
but there's no
presentation at these.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:16.810
It's just a different
data science leader

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:20.440
from the community who
joins me to answer questions

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:21.970
from the community.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:25.900
And so all the questions are
audience led from you all

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:31.360
and could be questions like,
how do I talk to executives?

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:35.320
Or how are you thinking
about hiring right now?

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:38.620
Should I have a centralized
or decentralized data

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:40.420
science team?

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:44.260
And these are every
Thursday at 12 Eastern time.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:47.570
I've included the link to
the calendar for these.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:51.290
If you ever want to add the
individual events or just

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:55.270
the whole series of
events to your calendar.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:57.610
This one is best
formatted for your phone,

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:01.120
but if you're on your desktop,
add an extra dash and type

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:04.270
calendar and it will
look a lot nicer too.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:08.180
But if you're also an organizer,
I just wanted to include that

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:12.040
the tool that I use here
is called "AddEvent".

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:13.100
I really like that.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.940
It makes it really
easy to share events

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.130
that people can just
put on their calendar

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:20.470
without having to give
me any information.

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:23.710
So find your Taco Tuesday.

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:27.010
Secondly, start
conversations across the bar.

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:31.240
So as I opened up
with that scene,

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:34.900
imagine the regular
sitting alone at the bar.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:38.710
My mom might walk over
to them and strike up

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:41.050
a conversation with them
and the couple sitting

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:43.630
a few seats down.

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:47.740
It might be about the band
that's playing next week

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:49.640
that she knows that
they both like.

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:52.300
Or it could be about a
special that's on the menu

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:55.960
that somebody else ordered
across the bar, too.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:00.557
The topic itself doesn't
really matter as much.

00:06:00.557 --> 00:06:02.140
It's just that she's
giving permission

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:05.320
for that interaction to
happen and kicking off

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:07.930
the conversation.

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:10.270
She might start the
conversation and then

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:12.490
have to go walk away
and run to the kitchen

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:16.450
and help with something or
make a drink behind the bar.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:18.700
But that person sitting
alone at the bar

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:23.140
might stay a little bit longer
because they now know somebody,

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:25.300
or they might come
back more regularly

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:28.580
because they feel a part
of the Sneakers community.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:34.550
I think one of the Sneakers
regulars said it best,

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:38.930
and this is a newspaper
article from a few years ago.

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:42.020
But Deb is someone who I've
known for many years now.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:45.580
She's filmed in
employee weddings.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:49.820
She actually made a video for
my high school volleyball team.

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:51.920
But Deb said it's the
kind of place where

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:56.300
you can go in alone, have
some food and a glass of wine,

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:00.390
and pretty soon you are a
part of the conversation.

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:05.220
I want people to feel this way
at RStudio community events

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:06.810
as well.

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:10.920
Minus the food and wine,
except for this week.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:14.460
But as community organizers,
we have a responsibility

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:19.320
to bring people together
based on shared interests.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:22.620
And I think it's important here
that these interests aren't

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:27.690
something that we ourselves
have to be experts on.

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:30.450
But we can go a
step further here

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:36.510
to help kick off conversations
with people at events as well.

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:40.290
I think one of my
favorite examples of this

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:42.780
comes from the data
science hangout, which

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:46.980
happens every Thursday
at 12 Eastern time.

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:51.510
But this particular week
featured Kristi Angel, data

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:54.410
science manager from Stitch Fix.

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:58.760
And leading up to that
session, a few people

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:02.360
had reached out to me on
LinkedIn or maybe at meetups

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:06.830
as well, and mentioned
that they're really

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:09.770
having a hard time
finding a new job

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:13.790
and they were hitting
roadblocks with recruiters

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:15.590
and not getting through
to a hiring manager

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:18.970
because they didn't
have a certain degree.

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:21.980
And so I had the
privilege of knowing this

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:24.410
as the organizer of
the group and getting

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:26.670
to talk with all these people.

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:28.980
So I was able to
ask this to Kristi

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:32.429
for them anonymously
just to get her insights

00:08:32.429 --> 00:08:36.110
and hear her experience.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:41.150
And I started to see in the
chat that everybody started

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:47.240
commenting with their own
degrees and their own non data

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:50.600
science degrees and a
roomful of data scientists

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:54.590
or a Zoom room full
of data scientists.

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:57.650
And it was just one
of those moments

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:02.420
that you just remember,
just seeing people

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:05.870
all supporting each
other and cheering

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:09.020
each other on for their
non data science degrees

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:14.440
and supporting people who are
running into this right now.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:18.590
So start those conversations
across the bar.

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:22.960
Third touch every table.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:24.670
So, at Sneakers

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:28.420
it's pretty much a guarantee
if you go in for dinner,

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:30.170
my mom will come by your table.

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:33.130
And Thank you for coming in.

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:34.910
If it's her night
to work, of course.

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:37.750
If it's her business partner,
Annie's time, night to work,

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:40.400
she'll be the one to come by.

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:42.740
And it doesn't matter
if you are a regular

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:47.780
or this is your first
time coming into Sneakers,

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:51.500
they'll stop by and say,
thank you for coming in

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:56.240
and ask where you're from,
how you're enjoying your meal,

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:58.160
or maybe reminiscing
with customers

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.520
who have become like family.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:03.570
Actually, I had no
idea this was a term

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:06.280
until preparing for this talk.

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:09.270
But table touching is a
real thing in the restaurant

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:11.640
industry, and there's
a lot of articles

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:14.970
about how
touching every table

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:16.760
can help create
a guest for life.

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:21.930
In the virtual world

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:27.870
I want to be able to stop by and
meet you at your table as well.

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:30.810
The way that I
like to do this is

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:33.420
when people join
a meetup or a data

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:37.530
Science Hangout is to
write down the names

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:41.070
of people who mentioned
they're joining for the very

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:42.870
first time.

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:48.570
Or maybe it's just a name that
I haven't seen join before,

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:51.210
but also write down
a few names of people

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:54.390
who have been coming
back regularly

00:10:54.390 --> 00:11:00.210
or names or faces that
I've been seeing on Zoom.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:03.310
And this is not possible, I
know, with hundreds of people.

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:06.420
But I don't think that's a
reason to not do it at all.

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:10.800
So I may say to my, oops,
I am I say to myself today

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:15.390
I'm going to write down the
names of five regulars and 5

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:19.650
new people, and I'll reach
out to them on LinkedIn

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:22.470
just to say Thank you,
Thank you for joining

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:27.330
or Thank you for the comment
that you put into the chat.

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:30.420
So it might be just a
few people that week,

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:36.900
but hopefully as we go, I get
to reach out to everybody.

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:39.300
So touch every table.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:43.200
And the last takeaway I
want to leave you with,

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:46.590
which also expands on
the last, last one.

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:51.150
Sometimes when you're going
by and visiting with a table,

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:54.300
you get a chance to learn more
about someone's experience

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:58.640
and how you can make
that a little bit better.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:02.750
But not only in what
they're telling you,

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:06.650
but in observing the unsaid.

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:09.320
So I think I've given
you enough restaurant

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:13.850
examples right now that I can
put you in the role of owner.

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:20.270
So let's say over here we
have our table of four who

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:23.510
are waiting for
trivia night to start.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:25.540
They all ordered
a round of drinks.

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:28.850
Three of them are finishing
up and you walk by their table

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:31.490
to check in on them.

00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:34.280
You notice, though,
that one of the drinks

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:36.025
is pushed over to the side.

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:40.700
Now you ask them how
their night's going.

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:42.660
They say everything's
great, but you

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:46.830
can tell they probably
didn't enjoy their drink as

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:49.530
much as they would have liked
to, by the way that it's

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:52.170
pushed over to the side.

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:54.660
So you look at that and
take it as an opportunity

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:58.320
to ask them if there's something
else that you could get them

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:00.450
and insist that you want
them to have something

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:03.870
that they'd enjoy.

00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:08.490
This doesn't have to just be
about untouched food or drinks.

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:11.430
It could also be about
stopping by a table

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:13.620
and noticing that it's
somebody's birthday

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:18.390
and noticing that before
they actually told you,

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:22.280
they're just noticing
the little things.

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:25.070
This dining experience
looks a little bit

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:28.160
different in a virtual world.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:30.720
So let me just take
you on a journey here.

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:34.850
So we'll look at my this
little stick figure here

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:39.500
and we can imagine it as
anyone, but we'll call him Bob.

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:45.610
So Bob joins a meetup event for
the first time and listens in.

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:48.670
Maybe Bob was on the list
of one of the five people

00:13:48.670 --> 00:13:50.660
that I wrote down
as a new person.

00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:52.690
Or maybe he wasn't.

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:58.780
He comes back again and joins
in listen only mode again.

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:01.930
A week later, he
comments in the chat

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:04.960
and then I notice
Bob on mute briefly.

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:11.320
It pops up to the top of my Zoom
but doesn't end up speaking.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:15.160
So I am not going to
say to Bob on LinkedIn,

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:19.780
hey, you join three
times and you never talk.

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:22.990
What can I do to make you
feel more comfortable?

00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:26.380
But I might say something
like, hey, Bob, Thanks

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:28.100
so much for joining today.

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:32.980
I loved your comment about
learning R as a SAS user.

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:36.790
I think that perspective
was incredibly helpful.

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:40.060
If you ever have any
suggestions or things

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:43.510
that we could be doing to
make this event more useful,

00:14:43.510 --> 00:14:46.440
please let me know.

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:49.720
And Bob might say,
Thanks for joining,

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:51.340
I had a great time.

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:55.350
I wanted to ask a
question, but I wasn't sure

00:14:55.350 --> 00:14:58.660
if it was the right format.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:02.680
And so I've had the opportunity
now to learn something

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:06.430
more about Bob's experience
by thanking him for coming

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:11.560
and by just noticing that
there was something there

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:14.680
in the unsaid.

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:17.590
And so what Bob
does for me here is

00:15:17.590 --> 00:15:24.400
I have that opportunity to say,
did you oh, oh, gosh, this is.

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:26.530
There we go.

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:31.300
Did that you can ask
questions anonymously as well?

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:33.290
And so,

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:34.530
I can let him know,

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:37.830
this is a place for any
question to be asked.

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:39.770
But there's
something more there.

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:41.450
It points out to
me that I didn't

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:46.250
make it clear, maybe in the
beginning of the presentation

00:15:46.250 --> 00:15:49.880
that people could ask
questions anonymously.

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:54.720
And if I said that once,
maybe that's not enough.

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:57.140
Because if somebody joined
a few minutes later,

00:15:57.140 --> 00:15:58.880
they would have missed that.

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:00.740
So it lets me know
that that's something I

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:05.250
should put in the chat as well.

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:10.950
So a month later, maybe he keeps
coming back and keeps joining

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:14.920
and eventually presents
at the meetup as well.

00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:18.130
I think this is one of my
favorite things about community

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:20.080
building.

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:24.150
Is seeing somebody come
for the very first time.

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:26.850
And then starting to
get to know people

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:29.610
and you see them like
being friends on Twitter

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:32.700
or commenting on each
other's LinkedIn posts.

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:36.000
And then eventually that
person being the one presenting

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:37.085
at the event too.

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:46.790
So today I've given
you four key takeaways

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:51.020
that I've learned from
growing up at Sneakers.

00:16:51.020 --> 00:16:54.020
But there are so many
more tips out there

00:16:54.020 --> 00:16:57.140
from amazing community
builders and community

00:16:57.140 --> 00:17:00.340
organizers like you all.

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:02.890
I know there are so many
here in the audience

00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:07.660
today at the conference and
watching virtually as well.

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:09.280
So I wanted to take
this opportunity

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:13.250
to share this resource as well.

00:17:13.250 --> 00:17:14.180
A few months ago.

00:17:14.180 --> 00:17:15.430
I guess it's not that new now.

00:17:15.430 --> 00:17:23.630
A few months ago, I helped build
the site, RStudio.com/champion,

00:17:23.630 --> 00:17:25.430
and it was designed
to help people

00:17:25.430 --> 00:17:29.460
advocate for data science
at their own organizations.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:31.470
And when they're
facing roadblocks

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:35.010
to have a place to go, but
also included a section

00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:37.380
on community building.

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:42.330
And this includes a lot
of knowledge and resources

00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:45.900
from amazing community
builders out there and people

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:50.040
that I've met at other events.

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:54.600
There's also a feedback form on
the very bottom of this site,

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:59.370
if you scroll to it, where
you can share your own tips

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:00.830
and tricks.

00:18:00.830 --> 00:18:03.835
Or ask to see something else
that's maybe not there yet.

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:10.960
I just want to say Thank
you so much to all of you

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:12.950
for being here today.

00:18:12.950 --> 00:18:18.790
And it's amazing to see you
all at the conference, too.

00:18:18.790 --> 00:18:23.440
I want to say a big Thank you
to my mom, Rhonda Dempsey, who

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:27.910
I learned so much from and
continue to learn so much from.

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:32.410
Also, a huge congrats to her
and her business partner, Annie,

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:37.300
who are celebrating 38 years
in business at Sneakers

00:18:37.300 --> 00:18:38.535
cafe this week.

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:49.610
And also feel like in
case my mom and dad

00:18:49.610 --> 00:18:52.280
were watching to say happy
anniversary to them too,

00:18:52.280 --> 00:18:55.340
because today is their
actual anniversary.

00:18:55.340 --> 00:18:58.700
But just like Sneakers
would not be the same

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:00.650
without their community.

00:19:00.650 --> 00:19:04.430
R and RStudio and
soon to be Posit

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:08.510
would definitely not be the
same without all of you.

00:19:08.510 --> 00:19:10.970
Thank you so much
again for being here.

00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:13.610
If you have any questions,
I'd love to hear them.

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:17.060
I'd also put my LinkedIn
and Twitter up on the screen

00:19:17.060 --> 00:19:19.940
as well because I would
love to connect there.

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:23.600
LinkedIn is probably
the preference.

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:25.790
I'll also take questions
on the Discord channel

00:19:25.790 --> 00:19:27.690
communities of practice, too.

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:30.250
Thank you, everybody.

